      "writtenOn": "2024-02-14T17:43:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I followed with ABI team in b/324152549#comment17.\n\nMatthias explained why this might be happening:\n\n\nAn include can cause CRC changes due to type visibility. E.g.\n\n\n```\n# inc.h\n\nstruct ABC {\n   int a;\n   int b;\n};\n```\n\n\n```\n# source.c\n\n#include \"inc.h\"\n\nstruct ABC;\n\nvoid func(struct ABC* abc) {}\n```\n\nThe `#include` makes additional type information available that the CRC computation is able to expand. Something like\n\n```\nvoid func(struct UNKNOWN*) {}\n```\n\nvs.\n\n```\nvoid func(struct {int;int;}*) {}\n```\n\nand that yields in different CRC values for the symbols. The `#ifdef` hides it from the CRC calculation and keeps the value stable.",
      "parentUuid": "6692cba8_9080ab1e",
